# Local notes (template)

Copy to `.claude/CLAUDE.local.md`, which is gitignored, and fill in for **this machine**. Claude
reads it alongside `.claude/CLAUDE.md`; a human reading it gets the same orientation.

Everything here is environment, not project knowledge. If a fact would be true for another
contributor on another machine, it belongs in `.claude/CLAUDE.md` or `docs/` instead.

Delete the prompts you do not need — an empty section is worse than an absent one.

## Stones

Which stone is which, and what is loaded in each. Worth recording because it is the thing most
often assumed wrongly.

| stone | version | netldi | extent / dir | Grail? | Rowan? | what it is for |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| | | | | | | |

* Which one `.setenv` currently points at, and how to switch.
* `StnMaxSessions` per stone, if it is low enough to matter. `run-unit-tests.sh` holds up to seven
  logins concurrently, so a stone that also backs a running server can fail the forking suites for
  reasons that have nothing to do with the code.
* Any stone that needs native code turned off in its own `gem.conf`, or a non-default
  `GEMSTONE_NRS_ALL` / `GEMSTONE_SYS_CONF`.
* Any stone that is safe to break, and any that is not.

## Running servers

* Which ports are in use by a long-running server here, and how to stop and restart them.
* Whether a client (Claude Code, VS Code, Inspector) is configured against one of them, and under
  what name.
* **Confirm which gem actually owns a port before diagnosing odd behaviour** — `ss -ltnp` /
  `lsof -i`, then check the process's product version. A leftover router from another stone or
  another GemStone version will answer instead, and it looks exactly like a bug in this code.

## Test / remote hosts

* Any VM or remote host used for versions this machine cannot run, how to reach it, and what is
  installed there.
* How code gets there (this repo's tracked files only, never the working tree wholesale).
* Whether its clone's `origin` is the right remote.

## Identity provider

Only if you run one locally for the OAuth path.

* URL, realm, and where its own configuration and notes live.
* Which claim is configured as the user id (`MCP_USERID_CLAIM`) and which scope emits it.
* Anything that has to be re-applied after a stone restart — a registered JWT key, for instance,
  does not survive one.
* Anything that has to be re-applied after a client re-registers itself.

## Local paths

* Product trees available on this machine, and their versions.
* Where extents, logs and certs live.
* Where this checkout is. Nothing in the repository should depend on that path; if something does,
  it is a bug worth fixing rather than recording.

## Machine quirks

Things that are true of this computer and would confuse someone else — a shell version with its own
gotchas, a browser that stops handling URL opens after long uptime, a timezone mismatch between
logs, an editor that mangles something.
